// ===========================================================================
// [C7] Spend circuit -- periodic column constants
// ===========================================================================
//
// Generated verbatim by `emit_circuit_7_periodic_coeffs`
// (stark/src/compact.rs). Do not hand-edit: re-run the emitter.
//
//     cargo test -p p01-stark --lib emit_circuit_7_periodic_coeffs \
//         -- --ignored --nocapture
//
// THREE SHAPES, ON PURPOSE, AND THE SHAPES ARE THE POINT.
//
//   * seven `[u64; 32]`  -- stride-16 columns, COMPRESSED. The shipped
//     `eval_periodic_stride16_at_z` (verify.rs) takes `&[u64; 512]` and reads
//     exactly 32 of its entries; the other 480 are provably zero and still
//     occupy 3,840 bytes of rodata. Seven columns x 3,840 = 26,880 bytes that
//     buy nothing. These are read by `eval_periodic_compressed32_at_z`.
//   * four row indices  -- one-hot columns get NO array at all. They are
//     evaluated by `eval_one_hot_lagrange(g^k, ..)`, which needs the exponent
//     and three multiplications.
//   * two `[u64; 512]`  -- the only genuinely dense columns, `active` and
//     `not_boundary_active`. 8,192 of C7's 9,984 bytes are these two.
//
// MEASURED 2026-08-24: the seven compressed tables are BYTE-IDENTICAL to
// `C3_*_PERIODIC16` in `periodic_ext_consts.rs`. They are still emitted
// separately rather than aliased, and the equality is pinned by a test
// instead. Aliasing would make a C3 rebake silently move C7; a pinned
// equality makes it go red. 1,792 bytes for an independent derivation is the
// same trade this crate already makes elsewhere.

